WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.240
Casual fans of Wonder Woman may have been
intrigued or confused by the armored outfit

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.380
the hero wears in her solo movie. After all,
it's a pretty far cry from the classic look

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.620
Lynda Carter sported back in the '70s. But
comic book fans know that Wonder Woman's costume

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.610
has been in constant flux for the past 75
years. Why? Here's a look at the untold truth

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.720
of Wonder Woman's costume.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.539
The original costume

00:00:22.539 --> 00:00:27.619
When Wonder Woman first appeared in All-Star
Comics #8 in 1941, she was already recognizable

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:32.119
as the iconic hero we know today. Inspired
by patriotic drawings of Lady Liberty from

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.910
the Sufragette movement and World War I, artist
H. G. Peter and writer William Moulton Marston

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:41.360
came up with a look featuring a gold tiara,
bracelets, red strapless top decorated with

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.390
a golden eagle, red boots, and blue culottes
spangled with white stars. Yes, that's right,

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.380
it's not a skirt — it's culottes.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.171
So why does Wonder Woman dress like the American
flag if she's not even from America? Because

00:00:52.171 --> 00:00:55.829
Wonder Woman's mom, Queen Hippolyta, cleverly
designed the costume to make her daughter

00:00:55.829 --> 00:01:00.789
more popular and appealing to Americans. Now
there's a savvy public relations move!

00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:02.980
A sanitized post-war version

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:08.119
When Marston died in 1947, the radical feminist
subtext of Wonder Woman died with him, killed

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:12.159
off by new writer Bob Kanigher. Diana soon
embraced traditional gender roles for the

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:16.920
1950s, longing to marry Steve Trevor and settle
down as a housewife. Her costume changed to

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.540
reflect her new values: her boots were replaced
with ballet slippers, her hair was pulled

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.040
back in a more conservative 'do, and her swimsuit
like costume became more demure, carefully

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.100
covering her cleavage and upper thighs.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.689
No costume, just fashion

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:34.440
In the late 1960s, Wonder Woman underwent
a drastic makeover, losing her superpowers

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.259
and her star-spangled costume to become a
globe-trotting action hero in the mold of

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.630
Emma Peel, the popular character played on
the British TV series The Avengers. Not only

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:47.370
was the new, mod Diana a fashion plate in
her own right, modeling a line of sleek jumpsuits

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.329
befitting an international adventurer, she
even opened a fashion boutique to help other

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:53.840
women find their own inner Wonder Woman.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.200
The new look Wonder Woman ended in 1972 when
Gloria Steinem featured Wonder Woman on the

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.200
cover of the first issue of Ms. Magazine and
successfully pressured DC Comics into returning

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.740
her to her superpowered roots.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.740
A new logo

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.989
Diana's costume received an important update
in 1982. DC President Jenette Kahn wanted

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:15.730
to give Wonder Woman a distinctive logo that
could be used for trademark and licensing

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.879
purposes. That eagle on her chest was nice
and patriotic, but it was also very generic,

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:23.939
unlike the logos for heroes like Superman
and Batman. Graphic designer Milton Glaser

00:02:23.939 --> 00:02:28.769
created a new double-W logo with wings. In
the comics, Diana is given this new design

00:02:28.769 --> 00:02:33.390
by members of a real-world charity organization
called the Wonder Woman Foundation, and decides

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.590
to wear it to help raise awareness of social
issues facing women around the world. Versions

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.560
of this logo have been in use ever since.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.109
A different Diana

00:02:42.109 --> 00:02:47.090
After the DC Universe was rebooted in the
Crisis on Infinite Earths crossover, writer/artist

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.629
George Pérez was brought in to relaunch Wonder
Woman in 1987. Though he left the costume

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:55.189
mostly the same, he gave it a new background
to explain exactly why it looks like an American

00:02:55.189 --> 00:02:59.620
flag. In this new origin story, Steve Trevor's
mother Diana crashed her plane on the hidden

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:04.340
island of Themyscira in the 1940s and died
helping the Amazons defeat a mystical threat

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.859
from the underworld. To honor her legacy,
Queen Hippolyta named her daughter after Diana

00:03:08.859 --> 00:03:12.120
Trevor, and designed a ceremonial suit of
armor based on the Women in the Air Force

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.129
patches on Trevor's flight jacket. That armor
would eventually become the basis for the

00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:18.870
outfit we know and love from the movies.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.499
Going full '90s

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:24.670
In the 1990s, the big trend in the comics
was replacing old heroes with newcomers. For

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:29.099
Diana, that meant she lost the title of Wonder
Woman to another Amazon named Artemis. Diana

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.329
kept up the good fight, of course, but she
did it wearing biker shorts, a strappy bikini

00:03:33.329 --> 00:03:38.010
top, a bolero jacket, and no less than three
belts. She even got a shorter haircut with

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:42.530
bangs to complete the extreme aesthetic. Naturally,
she got her title and the costume that goes

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:47.310
with it back after a few months, and her hair
even grew back out as soon as she put it on.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.249
Thank the gods!

00:03:49.249 --> 00:03:50.680
Hippolyta's sword

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:55.059
A few years later, Diana again stopped being
Wonder Woman and her lookalike mother Hippolyta

00:03:55.059 --> 00:03:59.400
took her place. Hippolyta's Wonder Woman costume
brought back the classic eagle, but made it

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.390
more like armor than a simple insignia. Meanwhile,
the W logo moved to her belt. She also wore

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:09.469
a skirt and carried a sword and shield instead
of a lasso. Though Hippolyta's time as Wonder

00:04:09.469 --> 00:04:13.459
Woman has largely been forgotten, the design
changes made by artist John Byrne have had

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:16.590
a lasting impact on Wonder Woman's look.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:17.780
Donna's armor

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.210
The Infinite Crisis crossover shook up the
DC Universe in 2006 — not least with another

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.651
new Wonder Woman in the form of Donna Troy,
Diana's adopted sister of sorts, and the hero

00:04:26.651 --> 00:04:30.550
formerly known as Wonder Girl. As the star
of a relaunched Wonder Woman series written

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:35.120
by Allan Heinberg with art by Terry and Rachel
Dodson, Donna wore a fully armored costume

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.150
with a gladiator skirt and a sword on her
belt.

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:39.680
Diana's pants

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.840
After dressing Wonder Woman in her classic
outfit for a number of years, DC Comics decided

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.800
to go totally off the rails in 2010 and revamped
her outfit to include a black leather jacket,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:52.860
a choker, and some yoga pants thrown in for
good measure. While some fans were happy to

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.069
see her wearing a more practical outfit instead
of a star-spangled swimsuit, the unfortunate

00:04:57.069 --> 00:05:00.960
'90s vibe created by artist Jim Lee brought
back too many traumatic memories. It didn't

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.699
end up mattering much either way, though,
because DC changed her look yet again just

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:06.599
a year later.

00:05:06.599 --> 00:05:07.830
Armored in silver

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:12.689
In 2011, DC continuity was completely rebooted,
and their entire line was relaunched with

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:17.979
52 new #1 issues. Diana's new look was once
again designed by Jim Lee, but this time it

00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:22.490
hewed closer to the original look, albeit
with some significant alterations. All traces

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.419
of yellow and gold in the costume were replaced
with silver instead, and the red on her top

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:30.490
was textured to make it look more like armor
than fabric. This costume also included a

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.610
sword — which has been standard for all
Wonder Woman costumes since — and for some

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.090
reason another choker.

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:38.479
The movie look

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:43.050
We caught our first glimpse of Gal Gadot as
Wonder Woman in July of 2014, in a promo image

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:48.060
for Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice, but
since then fans have had plenty of opportunity

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.909
to see the new cinematic look in action.

00:05:49.909 --> 00:05:53.409
The top is dark red and armored, while the
bottom is a blue gladiator skirt. She has

00:05:53.409 --> 00:05:57.719
the W logo on her belt, like Hippolyta's costume,
although there's another W layered into the

00:05:57.719 --> 00:06:02.040
eagle design on her chest. Her tiara points
down rather than up as it traditionally has

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.040
in the comics. And of course, she carries
a sword and a shield, in addition to the magic

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:10.809
lasso. It all adds up to one of the most instantly
iconic looks in Wonder Woman — and superhero

00:06:10.809 --> 00:06:11.809
— history.

00:06:11.809 --> 00:06:16.000
In 2016, DC once again rebooted their line
of comics, introducing another new costume

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.930
for Wonder Woman. This time, however, artist
Tony Daniel took the popular look from the

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:24.500
movies and used it as the basis for the design,
ensuring that fans of both the comics and

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.360
the films won't be confused — or disappointed.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.319
No matter what she's wearing, one thing is
for sure: she's a wonder, that Wonder Woman...

00:06:32.319 --> 00:06:35.389
Thanks for watching! Click the Looper icon
to subscribe to our YouTube channel. Plus

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:37.569
check out all this cool stuff we know you'll
love, too!

